course: dictionary
lesson: 6239
title: Mocking and unkind words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mocking means laughing at someone in an unkind way, often by copying how they talk or move. It is not the same as a friendly joke. A joke makes everyone laugh, including the person it is about. Mocking makes that person feel small.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «She put on a mocking voice and copied the way Sam walked.» The words copied and voice tell you that someone is being made fun of, and the feeling is unkind. Ask yourself: does the person being laughed at feel good or hurt?
explain: As an adjective, mocking describes a voice, smile or look. «He gave a mocking smile when I missed the ball.» A mocking smile is not kind. It shows the smiler is enjoying someone else looking foolish. Words like mocking tell you what a character feels and wants.
explain: «Jewish people have suffered terrible hatred... for a child today this means learning the truth, never joining in mocking, and standing up for Jewish friends.» Here mocking is listed with things we must not do. Standing up for a friend is the opposite of mocking them.
fact: Mocking means laughing at someone or something in an unkind way.
fact: «Mocking» can describe a voice, smile or look that shows unkind fun.
fact: A mockingbird is a bird that copies other birds' songs.
fact: Friendly jokes make everyone smile, but mocking hurts someone's feelings.
fact: In stories, a mocking laugh often shows that a character is unkind, proud or a villain.
exercise: «She gave a mocking smile when Ali fell over.» What does mocking describe here? | A kind smile to help Ali up | A loud sneeze | A sleepy yawn | *An unkind smile that laughs at someone
exercise: Which of these is mocking, not a friendly joke? | Sharing a silly story that makes you both giggle | Everyone in the group laughs together at a funny riddle | *Copying a classmate's walk so the others laugh at them
exercise: What is a mockingbird? | A bird that lives under the sea | A bird that cannot fly | *A bird that copies other birds' songs
test: «Stop mocking your little brother.» What does stop mocking mean? | *To stop laughing at him in an unkind way | To start singing loudly | To copy a bird's song | To run away from a friend
test: Which sentence uses mocking as an adjective? | *The mocking tone made the children go quiet | She mocks her friends every day | They laughed at the bird singing at dawn
exam: Why is mocking different from a friendly joke? | Mocking is always funnier than a joke | A joke is only for adults, and mocking is for children | Mocking and jokes are exactly the same thing | *Mocking hurts someone's feelings, while a friendly joke makes everyone smile
